# AdvCpp Standards

Codes in parentheses are rubric codes from `docs/review-error-codes.md`. No ex3-specific review guideline
has been published; the ex1/ex2 rubric still applies.

## Memory and lifetime

- No `malloc`/`free`; no raw `new`/`delete` (assignment 3 forbids them outright). RAII and smart pointers.
- Prefer `unique_ptr`. Use `shared_ptr` only when ownership is genuinely shared and the lifetime is unknown.
- Classes owning dynamic memory follow the rule of 3/5 (e13).
- Plugin lifetime: no object created from a `.so` may outlive its `dlclose`
  (`.cursor/rules/plugin-architecture.mdc`).

## API design

- `const` methods wherever state is not mutated (e07).
- Pass inputs as `const&`; avoid unnecessary copies (e06).
- Lean public APIs — no method not required by an interface or the assignment (e08).
- No exposed internal storage; no getter returning a backing container (e22).
- Error handlers take structured `ErrorRef` / enums, not ad-hoc diagnostic strings (e05).

## Structure

- Short functions, single responsibility (e09).
- Unrelated classes not grouped in one header (e01); don't over-split either (e02).
- Minimal `#include`s; forward-declare when possible; keep the include DAG acyclic (e17).
- Free helpers go in an anonymous namespace in the `.cpp`, not the public class API.
- No duplicate logic across components (e10). In ex3 this is easy to get wrong: shared logic goes in
  `UserCommon/`, not copy-pasted into two projects.

## Values

- No magic numbers — named constants (e23). This includes retry counts `N` from
  `docs/error-handling-matrix.md` and any thread/threshold tuning constants.
- No repeated expensive computation — compute once or cache (e21).

## Strong types

All distances and angles use the types from `common/include/Common/Units.h` — never a raw `double` for
cm/deg in an API. See `.cursor/rules/mp-units-strong-types.mdc`.

## Compiles clean

Every target builds with `-Wall -Wextra -Werror -pedantic` (the root `drone_warnings()` function). A
warning is a build failure, so don't leave unused parameters — use `[[maybe_unused]]` or omit the name.
